Points taken, but my response to that would be that the rider tattoos
need to be made riskier. A sure way to introduce things to the game
without wrecking play balance is to err on the side of caution and
make them so damn risky and/or useless that hardly anyone would use
them.

However, the massive diversity of potential situations you can find
yourself in in nethack means that sooner or later, some monkey with a
typewriter will find himself in a desperate position where those minor
benefits are actually worth the huge risks- I'm fainting and my god is
pissed off: The only thing nearby is a tattoo parlour- maybe if I get
a Tattoo of Artemis I'll be able to safely pray for help. The only way
off this level is on the other side of that Balrog and I just can't
take him on or get past him. If only I could walk through the walls.
Hey wait, there's a tattoo parlour. I'll get a tattoo of a Xorn, put
on that ring of polymorph and hope for the best...

Like I say, these situations are rare (especially among those players
smart enough to avoid such situations in the first place) but they do
come up, and it would be cool to throw a few more (potentially
booby-trapped) lifelines into the game. Once they are an established &
tested part of the game, you can look at making them more accessible.

To put it another way, nethack's great strength is the massive number
of interactions made possible by the sheer complexity and variety of
it. With this in mind, I'm of the opinion that as much stuff should be
thrown into the game as possible, to even further increase that
complexity and variety. Of course this raises big, ugly play balance
issues, so to counter this every addition should be introduced with
risks that far outweigh the potential benefits, and then gradually
brought down into the range of 'normal' risk:benefit as playtesting &
user feedback gradually explore and map their potential impacts upon
the game.

This the real reason I prefer Slashem- it just has so much more
*stuff*. True, a lot of it isn't particularly useful (I've never made
use of the game's toilets, for instance) but it's *there* for when I
do need it.

